Author: minh

--- log ---
2026-09-01T12:00Z claude created, from the author's scheduling discussion of 2026-09-01

--- body ---
## Context

The author asked for a theoretical framing of the conductor's scheduling:
how it guarantees completeness, freshness, and priority between lanes
(the factory metaphor: 15k pieces, three stages of increasing cost, some
pieces are batches). The discussion settled the following reading of
SPEC.md §5.2.3–5.2.5, and found exactly one gap.

What the design already has, restated in scheduling-theory terms:

- **Completeness is a reconciliation invariant, not a scheduler
  property.** The census diff re-derives "what should exist" every tick
  and re-queues anything missing (self-stabilization). The scheduler
  optimizes; the reconciler guarantees. Correct as designed.
- **The priority structure is a tree, not flat lanes.** Foreground
  (activity file) preempts everything; then the three §2.3 classes
  (metadata > notes/annotations > body) under strict priority — safe
  without arbitration constants because the upper classes are finite and
  short (metadata drains in ~8–10 min), so strict priority cannot
  starve; then, inside body only, band 0 > band 1.
- **Strict class priority already delivers cross-class freshness**: a
  fresh metadata change beats all body backfill with no dedicated lane.
- **The batch/monster problem is solved per-item by the bands, which is
  strictly better than a per-lane split by item size** (a "collections"
  lane would delay the monster's head too; banding indexes every item's
  head early). A proposed 4-classes × fresh/backfill = 8 flat lanes was
  examined and rejected on this ground: the tree needs one weighted
  arbitration where 8 flat lanes would need seven.
- **The micro-batch is the schedulable unit**; band/lane order is
  re-evaluated between micro-batches, which is what makes preemption and
  the band cap effective against a 15 000-page PDF.
- **Scheduler state lives in the ledger** (queues as rows, priority as
  ORDER BY, lease, claims, cursors); the only ephemeral state (current
  micro-batch, activity file, 30 s memoized probe) is crash-disposable.

## The gap

Inside the body class, nothing arbitrates between freshly-changed items
and the initial backfill. Freshness-optimal service is LCFS; fair
completeness is FIFO; a single queue cannot do both, and strict
fresh-first would starve backfill under sustained arrival. The standard
remedy is deficit round robin at a fixed ratio (serve k fresh
micro-batches per 1 backfill, k ≈ 3–4), which makes the fresh-lane
traversal bound computable: time ≤ (fresh-lane size × micro-batch cost)
/ (fresh share of the bottleneck). Stateless implementation available:
deterministic draw on the micro-batch counter (k fresh, 1 backfill,
modulo k+1) — no deficit counter needed in the ledger.

Refinement (author, 2026-09-01): the micro-batch constant is a TIME
budget, not a size. Batch size trades throughput (amortizing fixed
kernel/transfer costs — dominant on GPU, so optimal size is 10–100x the
CPU's) against preemption latency (queues, activity file and lease are
only re-checked between batches). So ratify one quantum (~1 s) and
derive the size adaptively per device: B <- B x (target / observed
duration), EWMA-smoothed, clamped by a fixed-cost floor and a memory
ceiling (VRAM on GPU; consistent with the per-device rung ruling). The
invariant "yields every ~1 s" then holds on any hardware, and R35's
minute stays discovery-dominated: tick <= 60 s, then fresh-lane
traversal of a few quanta. The only threat to the bound remains the one
unit with no internal boundary, the extract stage's whole-document GET.

Adjacent, not the same question: ticket 0080 asks what else has to hold
per item inside the body tier. The fresh-against-backfill level arbitrates
between two populations of items; 0080 is about one item's own ranges.

Secondary, from the #39 postmortem (SYNC.md): upstream's fix chose
extraction concurrency by observed degradation of the serving API.
Our extract worker is already sequential; adopting a latency-observed
back-off on top (slow down before Zotero stops answering, not only on
error) is worth a look while specifying the scheduling section.

## Actions

1. Put the lane-tree reading above into SPEC.md's scheduling prose
   (§5.2.3/§5.2.5), so the priority structure is named once rather than
   implied by three separate mechanisms.
2. Draft the fresh/backfill arbitration as an awaiting-ratification
   question in DECISIONS.md, with the recommended default and the
   drain figure it buys.
3. Decide (same ruling or a separate one) whether the extract worker
   gains a latency-observed back-off toward the local API.

## Verification

- [ ] SPEC.md names the priority tree in one place, and §5.2.3 points at
      it rather than restating it.
- [ ] DECISIONS.md carries the arbitration question with a recommended
      constant, marked awaiting ratification.
- [ ] make check passes — the guard list is the Makefile's `check` target
      (deps, ruff, figures, models, names, progress, erg check, pytest).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The lane tree is stated once in SPEC.md and the three constants it
      needs are drafted in DECISIONS.md's awaiting-ratification section,
      none of them presented as ruled. Ratification is the author's and is
      tracked by those entries, not by this ticket.
